in our series school matters we're  looking at how the pandemic is affecting  classroom results about two-thirds of  american students are using  some form of remote learning but some  new academic performance numbers suggest  the disruption to in-person education  is taking a heavy toll on many of them  meg oliver spoke to families in  different states and shows us  how they're coping meg good morning  anthony good morning students here in  montclair new jersey haven't seen the  inside of a classroom in more than  eight months but across the country  about 40 percent of districts fall into  that category  and the students grades tell a sad story  ericia oates is a single mom of four  children in east tampa florida  when he gets sick it's a whole other  level she chose all remote to protect  the health of her oldest son who suffers  from epilepsy  but her fifteen-year-old daughter is  struggling  has she ever failed a grade before she  has not  and this fall she made a d the last nine  weeks on  hp world history and she was freaking  out  that has never happened before how is  that affecting you it stressing me out  as well because my daughter has never  struggled  and to have her feel like she's being  defeated  is a problem it's a problem happening to  students around the country  in houston one of the largest school  districts in the nation  the number of students with failing  grades is exploding  during the fall of this year 42 percent  of students received  one or more f's in the first grading  period which was  100 percent virtual last year only 26  percent  fell into that category these children  are struggling to read struggling to do  math  christina quintero has two children in  houston school district  she says her first grade daughter feels  defeated  is she getting down on herself oh she's  very  critical very critical in her academics  because she just wants to do better she  wants to be able to  show that to her teacher and be proud of  herself we know that there has been a  significant loss of learning  but i tell you we're less concerned  about that than we are  about the social emotional factors dan  dominic  is the executive director of the  american association of school  administrators  and served as a superintendent for  nearly 30 years  we're seeing an increase in the stress  that students are feeling the  the emotional impact that this is having  on them we're carefully tracking suicide  rates  which is a major factor so we are more  concerned right now about the emotional  well-being of our students than we are  about their academic  loss between march and october of this  year  numbers from the cdc show mental health  related  emergency room visits increased nearly  28 percent for children  ages 5 to 17 compared to the same period  last year and grades in north carolina  paint a bleak picture as well  in wilson county 46 percent of students  in grades 3 through 12  fell to class this fall while learning  virtually that is more than  double from the same period last year we  made an early decision to award  incompletes to students who are trying  to make progress what weren't where they  were supposed to be  because it sends a signal to finish as  opposed to an f  in cleveland 42 percent of the students  live below the poverty line  superintendent eric gordon says 20  percent of cleveland metro students  had incompletes in the first marking  period  is it fair to have traditional grading  in all remote learning  by using an abc or incomplete strategy  we're actually moving towards does the  student know what we want them to know  before we move on  or do they need to finish that learning  so i think it's just another opportunity  for us to question  our past strategies and think about how  to make a better system when we come out  of this pandemic if things don't improve  for alicia's daughter she will send her  back to class for in person  and sent her son to live with her mom  the last time my son had a seizure  he he almost didn't make it so it's more  of  life or death for him because the  numbers in hillsborough county are  rising  so for safety that's one of the options  that we've been wearing  as parents face these difficult  decisions cleveland superintendent told  me we're unfairly measuring students  during a time  no one could have imagined they're  already discussing ways to close the gap  post pandemic whether that involves  summer school  night classes or even weekend camps tony  it's going to take a lot meg thank you  very much
 

